WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.562 --> 00:00:12.770
SAILESH KRISHNAMURTHY: Hello,
welcome to this session

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:15.080
on innovating with
transformative databases

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.240
for an always-on digital world.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:21.620
My name is Sailesh
Krishnamurthy,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.890
and I lead engineering for cloud
native databases at Google.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:28.490
Today I am thrilled to
have Hari Ramamurthy, who

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.220
is a technology fellow at
the Home Depot join us.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.490
Hari will share how the Home
Depot uses Google's cloud

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:37.400
native databases to
transform their business

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.263
in the modern digital world.

00:00:41.263 --> 00:00:42.680
We'll begin today
by talking about

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:46.410
transformative capabilities
and why they're so important.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.870
Next we'll review our
transformative databases,

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.260
Spanner, Bigtable,
and Firestore,

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.900
and the new innovations we are
bringing to this portfolio.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.290
And then we'll hear from
Hari on how the Home

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:01.030
Depot uses these databases.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:02.860
Today's innovators
are disrupting

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.890
traditional industries by
using data and software

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.550
to deliver personalized and
seamless digital experiences

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.070
across devices and channels.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:15.640
Modern conveniences, like
buy online, pickup in-store,

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.580
instant digital payments,
real time ride ridesharing

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.190
requests, and other
marketplace services

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:24.280
have raised the bar in consumer
experiences with the respect

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.620
to the size and the
complexity of the problem,

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:30.880
or even how regulated
the industry is.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.130
These disruptive
experiences require

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.950
sophisticated and
complex applications

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.220
that need to serve millions
of users in a reliable

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.380
and continuously
available fashion,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.340
skillfully handle viral growth
and unpredictable spikes

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.160
in demand without
missing a beat,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.380
and securely deal
with bad actors,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.190
and comply with stringent
regulatory regimes.

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:57.100
Speed is critical, and
you need the flexibility

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.670
to iterate quickly and deliver
innovative and differentiated

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:04.590
experiences for your customers.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:09.300
The key to scalably and securely
serving millions of users

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.480
is an operational database
that'll power your applications

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.010
and run your business.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.820
You need databases with
transformative capabilities.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:20.790
And the new table stakes are an
availability SLA of five nines,

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:24.660
with zero maintenance downtime,
and automatic failure recovery.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.750
That's at most five minutes
of downtime every year.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.690
Online and unlimited
scaling and zero

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:34.046
touch global and
multi-region deployments.

00:02:34.046 --> 00:02:36.490
And most important,
the highest security,

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:41.920
complying with the industry's
most demanding standards.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.090
At Google Cloud
we offer not one,

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.150
but three
transformative databases

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.890
that provide the
capabilities that you

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:51.170
need to deliver innovative
customer experiences.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:54.220
These are Spanner,
Bigtable, and Firestore.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.380
And through these
databases, you get

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:59.740
to access the very same
groundbreaking technologies

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.770
that power the Google apps that
we all love and use every day,

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:05.380
Search, YouTube, Gmail, Maps.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.810
Each of these are used by
billions of users worldwide.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.120
And while data is born
in operational databases,

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.910
these are only the first
part of a bigger journey.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:18.480
Our database services are
deeply integrated with a larger

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.810
ecosystem of market leading
data and AI services that

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.430
are part of Google Cloud.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:27.090
These include services like
BigQuery, GKE, DataFlow,

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.520
DataStream, Pub/Sub, Cloud
Functions, and so on.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.160
And these services
help you break down

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.340
your operational silos,
build data pipelines,

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.390
generate real time insights, and
make better business decisions.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.930
In 2021 we delivered many
new features and enhancements

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.940
across the entire portfolio.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:47.860
Some of the more
notable ones include

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.740
customer managed encryption
keys, data access audit

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:53.440
logging, key visualizers
for observability,

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.870
and an availability
SLA of up to five nines

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:59.870
for Bigtable, matching that
of Spanner and Firestore.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:01.750
And today we are
excited to share

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.960
how we are raising the
bar yet again with more

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:06.280
new innovations.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.360
Let's take a closer look.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.000
Spanner is a fully managed
relational database

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.670
with global scale,
asset transactions,

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.339
and full SQL support,
strong consistency

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:20.370
across regions and
continents, high availability,

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:23.730
up to five nines with
zero scheduled downtime.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.520
To give you a sense of the
magnitude of its scale,

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.020
Spanner processes over a billion
requests per second at peak.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.930
There's never, ever been
a relational database

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.180
with the scale of Spanner.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.520
And now we want to
democratize Spanner and make

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.410
it available to everyone.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:45.220
Earlier this year we announced
granular instance sizing, which

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.260
is available in
preview, and works

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:51.190
based on a new concept of
Processing Units, or PUs.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.100
A node is made of 1,000
PUs, and you can now

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.530
get started by
commissioning 100 PUs.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:01.150
You can scale up in steps of
100 PUs without any downtime,

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:05.540
going from 100, 200, 300,
all the way to 1,000 PUs,

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.790
which is one node.

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:09.280
And you can then scale
out by adding capacity

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:14.020
in increments of 1,000 PUs,
1,000, 2,000, 3,000, or a node

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:15.730
at a time.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:17.920
With granular instance
configurations,

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.495
you will be able
to provision one

00:05:19.495 --> 00:05:22.960
tenth of a current node
for one tenth the price,

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:27.560
and can now get started with
Spanner for just $65 a month.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:29.560
We think this is a game
changer for customers

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.080
who need smaller instances
for smaller and medium sized

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.990
workloads, and also
unlocks creativity

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:37.510
and innovation for smaller
teams on tight budgets.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.590
PostgreSQL has emerged today as
the de facto industry standard

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:46.170
for relational
databases as businesses

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.030
accelerate to the cloud.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:50.760
And today I am excited to
announce Cloud Spanner's

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.730
Postgres interface, which
further democratizes access

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:57.330
to Spanner for millions
of developers and ISPs.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.880
This interface combines
the power and reliability

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.900
of Spanner with the familiarity
and portability of Postgres.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.090
With the Postgres
interface, enterprises

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:07.950
looking to standardize
on Postgres

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.350
can now take advantage of
Spanner's global scale,

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:13.620
five nines availability,
and strong consistency using

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:17.310
the skills and tools of the
popular and ubiquitous Postgres

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.690
ecosystem.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.150
Spanner's Postgres interface
is now available in preview,

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:22.590
and we'd love your feedback.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:24.990
If you are interested,
please sign up on the URL

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:28.090
listed on the slide.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:32.260
Bigtable is a fully managed,
scalable, NoSQL database

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:35.560
for large, operational,
and analytical workloads,

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.450
with an industry
leading availability

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:39.700
SLA of up to five nines.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:43.300
It's ideal for apps that require
consistent, single digit,

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.850
millisecond latencies,
and need to support

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:49.060
high throughput, millions
of requests per second.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:52.510
Bigtable scales seamlessly
from a first gigabyte

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:53.560
to petabyte scale.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:57.010
And in fact, Bigtable
has more than 10 exabytes

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:58.690
of data under management.

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:00.940
It also integrates with
the Apache ecosystem

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:04.650
and supports the HBase API.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:08.160
And today I'm excited to share
that autoscaling is coming soon

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.300
to Bigtable.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.020
With autoscaling, Bigtable will
automatically add or remove

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:15.960
capacity in response
to the changing demands

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:17.100
of your workloads.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.740
So you only pay for the
capacity that you need.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:24.160
Autoscaling reduces management
overhead, as your teams can now

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:25.660
spend more time
on your business,

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:29.870
and less time managing
your infrastructure.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:32.420
And we've also doubled the
storage units for Bigtable

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.380
so you can now store
more data cheaply.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:38.470
Bigtable nodes now support
twice as much storage capacity.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:41.590
From SSD you go from a limit
of two and a half terabytes

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.810
per node to a maximum of
five terabytes per node,

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:46.810
and for HDD, from 8
terabytes per node

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.850
to a maximum of 16
terabytes per node.

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:53.080
This is especially more cost
effective for batch workloads

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:57.660
that operate on large
amounts of data.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:01.830
Firestore is our fully managed,
scalable, and truly serverless

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:06.000
document database that lets
you easily and rapidly develop

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.790
rich mobile and
web applications.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:12.150
All this with an availability
SLA of up to five

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:15.990
nines that's achieved through
strongly consistent data

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:17.280
replication.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.620
Developers love Firestore
because of its ability

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.580
to serve both as a
document database

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:23.380
and as a backend
business service.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.470
It's therefore, no
surprise that Firestore

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.780
has built a thriving
developer community

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:32.280
with more than 250,000 monthly
active developers and Firestore

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:37.620
apps powering more than 750
million monthly active end

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:40.669
users using Firebase Auth.

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:43.100
You can develop and
deploy apps fast,

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.560
with direct connectivity
to the database,

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:47.240
no middle tier needed.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.300
These apps can have rich and
collaborative experiences

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:54.140
with built-in real time live
synchronization across devices,

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.840
as well as seamless
support for disconnected

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:57.800
or an offline mode.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:03.390
Firestore's developer
experience truly sets it apart.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:06.390
And we continue to invest
heavily in this area.

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:09.900
With Key Visualizer, customers
can quickly and visually

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.000
identify performance issues.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.950
With the latest
version of our web SDK,

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:17.310
we've added tree shaking,
which reduces the SDK footprint

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:19.110
by as much as 80%.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:22.770
A smaller SDK means faster
load times for your app.

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:26.700
Both Key Visualizer and the new
web SDK are available today.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:30.600
We also graduate the Unity
and C++ SDKs to general

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.400
availability in Q4.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.440
Both of these SDKs
make it easier

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.020
to develop mobile
games using Firestore.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:38.820
We're excited about
mobile gaming.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.970
As game developers have
told us how Firestore lets

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:46.880
them quickly build and
iterate on their games.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:48.560
In addition to
developer friendliness,

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:50.460
we've been focused
on making Firestore

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:54.200
more enterprise friendly, with
a strong commitment to security

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:55.820
and privacy.

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.220
Data access audit
logs in preview

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:00.800
lets privileged
administrators audit

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:05.900
all operations, admin, and
data on a Firestore database.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:10.115
Custom IAM in GA lets you
align Firestore permissions

00:10:10.115 --> 00:10:13.160
with your organization's
IAM roles.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:14.750
In addition to
these enhancements,

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:16.970
I'm excited to
announce new features.

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:19.400
With VPC service
controls customers

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:22.100
will be able to reduce
exfiltration risks

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:25.670
through network level security
controls that include Firestore

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:28.100
in a security perimeter.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:30.110
And with App Check,
customers will

00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:33.560
be able to ensure only signed
and pre-authorized apps

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:36.500
are allowed to make
requests to Firestone.

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:38.870
We'll roll out previews
for VPC service controls

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:42.350
and App Check in Q4.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:44.570
Let's now take a look
at real world examples

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:47.420
and hear from Hari on
how the Home Depot is

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:50.910
using these transformative
databases for a variety of use

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:51.410
cases.

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:56.370
HARI RAMAMURTHY:
Thank you, Sailesh.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:59.610
Home Depot is the largest
home improvement retailer

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:04.500
in the US with close to
2,000 stores in the US.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:09.450
But specifically in the context
of this last year that's

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:13.020
been so unprecedented,
the amount of stress

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:17.300
that we've been placing on
all our IT systems in general

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:19.080
has really been significant.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:23.040
And thanks to the Google
platform overall and many

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:25.470
of these databases, I think
our systems have actually

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:29.490
come out doing quite well during
these really challenging times.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:31.350
Some of the key
metrics, if you really

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:35.640
look at it in the course of
this really extraordinary period

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:41.340
that we are in, we're delighted
that we grew our business

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:44.840
in 2020 by $21 billion.

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:54.450
86% of the digital sales growth
and over $3.6 billion visits

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:57.600
to our website are some
of the key metrics that

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:02.130
underscore the criticality
of the technology platforms

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:07.470
and solutions that we've been
running through this period.

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:17.150
Let's look at Cloud Spanner and
its usage in some more detail.

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:21.620
There are multiple scenarios
in the organization,

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:24.460
especially in areas
like supply chain,

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:30.370
or order management, or loyalty
where it is really critical

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:36.130
that the system state
moves from one atomic,

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:43.120
ACID compliance state to another
consistent transactional state.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:45.910
And we need to do
this in addition

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:49.720
to those systems actually being
available in multiple data

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:52.360
centers and serving
traffic coming

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:58.630
from various regions
to these APIs

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:00.730
and the databases
that power them.

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:04.000
And this is really
where Spanner has

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:07.390
been a key player in
ensuring that we're

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:10.990
meeting the level
of service that

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:16.100
is expected of our customers.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:20.300
To look at Spanner
in some more detail,

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:24.290
I think the fact that
we, in these areas,

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:29.780
are able to deploy our software
on three or more data centers

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:32.210
and have really large
volumes of data--

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:36.050
I mean, the 10 TB is still
where we are currently

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:38.180
and we see that growing rapidly.

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:43.070
But be able to serve the
traffic that's coming through

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:47.540
in multiple data centers,
and have the system move

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:51.200
from that one consistent
transactional state to another

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:58.970
has been really key in making
sure that we are always

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:03.110
not only serving traffic
at extremely high volumes,

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:08.630
but we do not have to do it with
any fear about the consistency

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:11.330
with which the system
is actually performing.

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:15.890
In addition, capabilities
like secondary indices

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:18.980
has been really helpful
in some of these scenarios

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:22.310
in making sure that
we can access the data

00:14:22.310 --> 00:14:28.040
and reach the appropriate
pattern of usage,

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:30.320
depending on the context.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:32.990
The fact that we
are fundamentally

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:36.110
able to deploy these
applications that

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:40.340
have strong consistency
guarantees without needing

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:44.030
to worry about uptime,
and be able to serve

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:48.230
our customers in these
various geographical zones

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.300
has really been a
game changer for us.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:52.670
And it's something
that we've been

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:56.450
really excited to use
and employ as part

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:57.770
of our overall strategy.

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:02.880
On the other side
of the spectrum,

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:06.380
there are also multiple
scenarios where we may not

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:09.140
need the strong
consistency guarantees,

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:13.730
but we have extremely demanding
requirements with regards

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:17.430
to the response time
of the database,

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:19.910
as well as the scale
of the database.

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:22.070
And this is really
where Bigtable's

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:24.500
been a game changer for us.

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:28.820
A specific case in point
is on the website where

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:35.330
we employ personalized
experiences for our customers,

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:40.100
we've been able to tailor
these experiences at extremely

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:43.250
high volumes based
on the journey

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:47.360
that the customer has taken,
combine it with prior purchase

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:51.470
history, as well as both
static and dynamic rules

00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:56.120
that we've actually learned
based on customer shopping

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:57.110
behaviors.

00:15:57.110 --> 00:16:00.050
And some of these
requirements demand

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:02.840
that we easily scale
up to thousands

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:04.340
of transactions per second.

00:16:04.340 --> 00:16:07.100
And we've been able to do
that quite effortlessly

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:09.080
with Bigtable.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:13.010
In addition, there are scenarios
like inventory aggregation

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:16.820
where we really have to
bring together the inventory

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:21.470
picture across our network,
both the stores and the supply

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:25.140
chain, and be able to do this
in a matter of milliseconds

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:29.480
so that we are able to
appropriately promise

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:31.520
to our customers
the inventory that's

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.040
available in various locations.

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:40.640
And we're really glad that we
chose Bigtable as the platform

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:43.790
to bring some of
these experiences.

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:48.990
I think it's noteworthy that
in many of these scenarios,

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:53.840
we are dealing with certainly
thousands of transactions

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:56.660
per second during
regular processes,

00:16:56.660 --> 00:17:00.920
but in scenarios like
the recommendations,

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:05.000
there are scenarios where we
need to process certain batch

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:09.079
processes that may push up
the database's transaction

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:13.520
throughput to up to 450,000
transactions per second.

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:18.319
And we do this on a consistent
basis for short periods of time

00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:23.390
without needing to
employ a large cluster

00:17:23.390 --> 00:17:27.260
throughout the period, but
rather just bringing up

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:31.430
the increased number of
nodes when we need it,

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:35.630
and then going back to a
more regular sized cluster.

00:17:35.630 --> 00:17:40.430
As mentioned here, currently
that's typically from 10

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:42.500
to up to 30 nodes.

00:17:42.500 --> 00:17:47.150
And we're really excited about
having this type of elasticity

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.980
come to play.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:54.680
In addition, the fact that we're
able to serve these extremely

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:58.220
high volumes of
traffic without having

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:00.950
to worry about maintaining
these databases,

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:05.030
worrying about updates,
and be able to do it

00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:09.980
in that multi datacenter
set up that is almost

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:12.920
necessary for our
scales and volumes

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:16.640
has been truly a
game changer and has

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:19.070
allowed us to deal
with the type of spikes

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:22.700
that we have seen through
the course of this last year.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:30.680
Firestore is another
really interesting database

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:33.380
that we're doing
some prototypes with.

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:36.650
And the specific
capability of Firestore,

00:18:36.650 --> 00:18:40.880
with its offline
capabilities both from a read

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:45.740
as well as from a write
through cache standpoint

00:18:45.740 --> 00:18:47.510
is something that's
of great interest.

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:52.520
And there are certain scenarios
across our stores in home

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:57.200
where we really believe
that this capability could

00:18:57.200 --> 00:18:58.680
come in really handy.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.140
And we're currently
in the process

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:03.440
of prototyping this database.

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:06.500
And we're really excited
by what it has to offer.

